    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Adenosine-to-Inosine (A-to-I) RNA editing is a biological mechanism that produces targeted modifications to the nucleotide sequence of mRNA transcripts. The functional roles of many site-specific and tissue-specific editing events are unknown and require further investigation to uncover novel editing functions. Previous work has shown that mice and flies deficient in A-to-I RNA editing undergo seizures and neurodegeneration, but these studies fail to demonstrate the more nuanced roles that this mechanism plays in neurological regulation and animal behavior. Here, CRISPR-Cas9 recombinant technology and the UAS-Gal4 RNAi knockdown system were used to perturb editing at specific sites and throughout specific tissues to assess how targeted editing events are correlated with complex behaviors and neuronal gene expression in Drosophila melanogaster. Abnormal behaviors were not found in mutants with perturbed editing at targeted sites in the genes GluClα, para, qvr, sei, and RyR. However, flies with knockdown of editing in glial cells exhibited reduced climbing activity, which provided evidence that editing at the cellular level can regulate subtle behaviors. These findings indicated that complex cellular and phenotypic responses were linked to regulation of many editing sites within a specific tissue, rather than regulation of one editing site across many tissues, providing novel insights about the roles of A-to-I RNA editing in the generation of crucial neurological processes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
